# Waypoint configuration
# Copy this file to `.env` and adjust as needed. Everything here is OPTIONAL:
# without a .env, Waypoint stores its database inside the project directory.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The database location is normally set by WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR + WAYPOINT_DB_FILE
# (see the Database directory section below) — that pair is the single source of
# truth for BOTH a local run and Docker.
#
# WAYPOINT_DB is an optional explicit override for a LOCAL run only (Docker
# ignores it). If set, it wins over WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR. Leave it unset so local
# and the container resolve to the same database.
#   • Leave unset       → <WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR>/<WAYPOINT_DB_FILE>, else <root>/data.db
#   • Absolute path     → used exactly as given
#   • Relative path     → resolved from the project root
#WAYPOINT_DB=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Port for the REST API + web UI (default 4000). The Vite dev proxy reads this
# too, so the dashboard keeps talking to the API if you change it.
PORT=4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dashboard
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Status filters shown as chips on the dashboard, comma-separated. "all" is a
# virtual filter that lists everything except archived projects. Valid project
# statuses are: active, paused, done, archived.
#   Default: all,active,paused,done,archived
#WAYPOINT_FILTERS=all,active,paused,done,archived
#
# Which filter is selected when the dashboard first loads. Must be one of the
# filters above.
#   Default: active
#WAYPOINT_DEFAULT_FILTER=active

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database directory (read by BOTH Waypoint locally AND docker-compose.yml)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The host folder that CONTAINS the SQLite file (data.db plus its -wal/-shm
# sidecars). This pair is the single source of truth for where data lives:
#   • Local run  → reads <WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR>/<WAYPOINT_DB_FILE> directly
#   • Docker     → bind-mounts the folder to /data and uses /data/<file>
# So a local `npm start`/CLI/MCP and `docker compose up` share one database.
#
#   • Leave WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR unset → local falls back to <root>/data.db, and
#     Docker uses a managed named volume "waypoint-data".
#   • Absolute path → that host folder is used (reuse an existing DB).
#   WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR=/Users/you/waypoint-data
#WAYPOINT_DATA_DIR=
#
# SQLite filename inside that directory.
#   Default: data.db
#WAYPOINT_DB_FILE=data.db
